%% ============================================================
%% F1: All dt>1 degrade
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F1: All Variable-Rate Configurations Degrade}
\label{sec:f1}

Every configuration that introduces $\dt > 1$ imagination degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (Figure~\ref{fig:learning_curves}). The baseline reaches 915.7 at 105K; fixed $\dt{=}2$ (006) peaks at 485.3 before collapsing to 132.9 ($14\%$ of baseline); fixed $\dt{=}4$ (007) never exceeds 176.9 ($19\%$); the learned gate (005) collapses to 98.8 ($11\%$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_learning_curves.pdf}
\caption{Learning curves. The baseline (black) reaches 915.7; all variable-rate configurations degrade.}
\label{fig:learning_curves}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
%% F2: Trained dt_embedding → nve > 1.5x
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F2: Trained Temporal Embedding Precedes Value Overestimation}
\label{sec:f2}

The factorial design isolates trained $\dt$-embedding as the strongest predictor. The \emph{primary} comparison, 008d (trained, subsample ON) vs.\ 008f (no embedding, subsample ON), differs only in embedding training, yielding $\nve = 4.12\times$ (eval 82.8) vs.\ $\nve \leq 1.69\times$ (eval 139.5--468.8$^f$). \emph{Secondary} comparisons against 008a/008c (unexposed embedding) co-vary subsample status and horizon alongside the embedding factor. The $\nve \geq 2.0\times$ threshold (35K+ window) classifies all $n{=}36$ configurations across two environments (9 CIVO-positive, 27 non-CIVO; AUROC${=}1.000$ [95\% bootstrap CI: 1.000--1.000, 10{,}000 stratified resamples], F1${=}1.000$; Table~\ref{tab:nve_loeo}; Figure~\ref{fig:nve_trajectory}).
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_nve_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{NVE trajectories. Trained $\dt$-embedding (red) exceeds the $2.0\times$ threshold in 3/5 seeds; no-embedding control (blue) remains near $1.0\times$. Three trajectory types: early collapse, late-onset collapse, and self-correction. Dotted line: 35K window onset.}
\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO onset (NVE $\geq 2.0\times$) in 3 of 5 Walker Walk seeds, degrading all five to below 30\% of baseline (IQM $148.8$, $n{=}5$; \citet{agarwal2021precipice}). Including Cheetah Run (\S\ref{sec:f6}), CIVO onset occurs in 5/7 6-DoF locomotion runs; 3 sustain collapse, one self-corrects, one shows NVE self-correction without policy recovery (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{Mechanism evidence.}
The RSSM reward head develops a $1.63\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ on Walker (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), but Cheetah shows no reward gap ($1.00\times$) despite higher CIVO severity ($8.27\times$); reward fidelity degradation is one transmission pathway but not necessary. The critic internalizes the bias: mean $V(s)/\text{MC-return}$ reaches $7.80\times$ (100\% overestimated) vs.\ $1.05\times$ for the control. Evaluation return collapses ${\sim}35$K steps before actor entropy rises (Appendix~\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Temporal ordering ($n{=}2$; correlational).}
Dense checkpoint analysis (12 points, 10K--65K) shows reward fidelity degradation precedes value overestimation: fidelity crosses $1.1\times$ at 20K while value-to-eval rises at ${\sim}$30K ($r{=}0.81$, $p{=}0.002$; Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_ordering}). A second seed confirms the pattern (lead ${\approx}$9K; Appendix~\ref{sec:timeseries_fidelity}). This temporal precedence is consistent with but does not establish causal direction.

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly necessary} for CIVO. Neither factor alone triggers it: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, peak NVE ${\leq}0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, peak NVE ${\leq}1.69\times$$^f$) both remain below $\tau{=}2.0\times$. Freezing the embedding eliminates CIVO across 10 seeds (6 Walker, 4 Cheetah; all NVE $< 2.0\times$; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}), confirmed by $2{\times}2$ verification (exp\_084--087, NVE $< 0.53\times$). Overall, trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in 5/7 6-DoF locomotion seeds vs.\ 0/16 frozen or removed controls ($p < 0.001$). Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). Details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}.

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO: all value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific, with frozen controls showing comparable peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}). CIVO is specific to moderate-complexity locomotion; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination eliminates CIVO.}
Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (exp\_008e\_v3; Appendix~\ref{sec:code_corrections}) yields 0/4 CIVO (peak NVE ${\leq}0.8\times$); three seeds achieve $64$--$94\%$ of baseline (mean $775.6$; Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). Multi-step imagination, not the trained embedding alone, is necessary for CIVO.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s1 ($\dt{=}1$ imagination), no CIVO onset (NVE ${\leq}\,0.6\times$, eval $94\%$ baseline).}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Full-SGS (exp\_018) detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$. Seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, NVE $1.66\times$; anecdotal, $n{=}1$); seed~2 collapses (0/2 elimination; Appendix~\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah confirms CIVO generalization (010b, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\times$); full-SGS yields 0/3 elimination. Removing the embedding eliminates CIVO on Cheetah (024b, NVE $< 0.2\times$). Targeted obs-decoder-only SGS (exp\_025/026) achieves 1/6 prevention (1/4 Walker, 0/2 Cheetah; Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}), confirming pathway redundancy: blocking any single channel shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).


%% ============================================================
%% Forward-pass interventions
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_results}

We test two forward-pass alternatives (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}). \textbf{RAVE} (value-level capping) fails because the EMA anchor tracks biased values during CIVO onset. \textbf{TCN} (representation-level noise) suppresses CIVO in 2/4 Walker seeds at $\sigma{=}0.2$ (NVE $< 1.0\times$, eval 45--91\%), but remaining seeds show runaway escalation, a binary outcome suggesting TCN gates CIVO on or off rather than attenuating it. Cross-environment transfer fails: the Cheetah effective dose ($\sigma{=}1.0$) erodes policy to 15\% of baseline (Appendix~\ref{sec:tcn_cross_env}). TCN is a \emph{diagnostic probe}, not a deployable mitigation.

%% ============================================================
%% Architecture-Level Ablation
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Architecture-Level Ablation}
\label{sec:arch_results}

\paragraph{Critic convergence in pure imagination.}
Across all dual-critic configurations ($n{=}7$), critics converge ($\rho > 0.999$) with pessimism at 3--4\% of the independent-critic bound (\S\ref{sec:dual_critic_method}), rendering pessimistic estimation ineffective. In a $2{\times}2$ factorial, min drives recovery (narrow dual-critic recovers fully, ${\sim}16$--$19\times$ ratio) while prevention requires min $+$ capacity (wide dual-critic, $\nve \leq 1.4\times$, 3/3 prevention). Cross-environment transfer is limited (Appendix~\ref{sec:dual_critic_appendix}).

%% ============================================================
%% Online CIVO Detection
%% ============================================================

\subsection{Online CIVO Detection}
\label{sec:detector}

A lightweight online detector monitors the value-to-evaluation ratio for superlinear growth (Algorithm~\ref{alg:civo_detector}, Appendix~\ref{sec:detector_appendix}). Applied to 14 runs across three environments, it achieves TPR${=}5/5$ and FPR${=}2/9$ ($22\%$), with predictive alerts in 2/5 cases. Larger-sample validation is needed.
